# The owner lane: a pull request touching any path below cannot merge without
# the operator's approving review (`protect-main` sets
# `require_code_owner_review`). Agents act as `brokdar-agent[bot]`
# (scripts/as-agent.sh), which is not a code owner, so no agent can clear this —
# not by approving, not by merging, not by being the author.
#
# What is here, and why: the paths where a plausible-looking change is a
# security or data incident, and the paths that define the gate itself — an
# agent PR must not be able to change what checks agent PRs.
#
# Every entry is a literal path, no globs, and `scripts/check-codeowners.sh`
# fails the commit if one stops matching a tracked file: a rename would
# otherwise drop its protection silently, with GitHub saying nothing.

# Auth and session: the single-user login, the session cookie, and where the
# guard is attached. `deps.py` only DEFINES `require_session`; which routers
# carry it is decided in `app/main.py` (the open router vs the guarded one), so
# moving a router across that line removes auth without touching an auth file.
/backend/app/api/session.py           @brokdar
/backend/app/api/deps.py              @brokdar
/backend/app/api/routes/auth.py       @brokdar
/backend/app/main.py                  @brokdar

# MCP authentication: bearer keys, scopes, the identity tools act as, and
# `mcp/main.py`, which attaches the key verifier to the server — dropping that
# argument leaves every tool open.
/backend/app/mcp/auth.py              @brokdar
/backend/app/mcp/identity.py          @brokdar
/backend/app/mcp/main.py              @brokdar

# What is exposed, and how it is deployed (the compose file and the proxy; the
# Dockerfiles and `.env.example` are not owned, see the end).
/caddy/                               @brokdar
/docker-compose.yml                   @brokdar

# The guards themselves: CI and repo settings (`.github/`, `setup-repo.sh`), the
# agent's identity and what it may run (`as-agent.sh`, `.claude/settings.json`,
# `.claude/hooks/`), the lane classifier and the merge breaker, and this file's
# checker — with their tests. Each of these decides whether an agent PR needs
# the operator or whether agents may merge at all.
/.github/                             @brokdar
/.claude/settings.json                @brokdar
/.claude/hooks/                       @brokdar
/scripts/as-agent.sh                  @brokdar
/scripts/as-agent.test.sh             @brokdar
/scripts/risk-tier.mjs                @brokdar
/scripts/risk-tier.test.mjs           @brokdar
/scripts/review-breaker.mjs           @brokdar
/scripts/review-breaker.test.mjs      @brokdar
/scripts/check-codeowners.sh          @brokdar
/scripts/check-codeowners.test.sh     @brokdar
/scripts/setup-repo.sh                @brokdar

# DELIBERATELY NOT OWNED, and the hole it leaves: `justfile` (its `gate` recipe),
# `.pre-commit-config.yaml`, `mise.toml`, `.agents/` (skill text agents follow),
# the Dockerfiles, `.env.example`, the other scripts, `backend/pyproject.toml`
# (the import-linter contracts, pytest options, ruff rules), `frontend/package.json`
# (the scripts the gate runs), the lint and test configs, `backend/app/core/config.py`
# (session cookie settings) and `backend/tests/`. An agent PR that edits one can
# weaken the check that judges agent PRs and merge without the operator. They are
# unowned because owning them sends every dependency bump and every new setting
# or test to the operator too; that trade is the operator's to make, not a default.
#
# Also not owned, by the operator's decision on 2026-11-10: migrations
# (`backend/app/persistence/alembic/`) and the guarded router list
# (`backend/app/api/routers.py`). Schema and new endpoints are routine
# development, and owning them sent almost every feature PR to the operator.
# What still stands between an agent and a bad migration: CI runs
# `alembic upgrade head` and `alembic check` against real Postgres, the
# migration heuristic (`scripts/check-migration-required.sh`) fails a model
# change without one, and docs/review/rollback.md says how to step back.
# A router added to the guarded list is behind the session guard by
# construction, and `test_router_protection.py` demands a 401 from every
# endpoint off its open list; making one public still needs `app/main.py`.
#
# And by the operator's decision on 2026-11-11, cutting the lane to the guards
# alone: `backend/schemathesis.toml` (every new endpoint adds a block there, so
# it sent nearly every feature PR to the operator), the rest of `.claude/`
# (skills, rules, agent definitions, workflows, plugins, the status line), and
# the build's helper scripts (`check-migration-required`, `check-api-schema-sync`,
# `ci-status`, `workflow-guards.test.mjs`). The hole it leaves: an agent PR can
# narrow the fuzzer, loosen a CI helper or rewrite a skill and merge without the
# operator — the same trade already made for `justfile` and `.pre-commit-config.yaml`
# above. CI itself (`.github/workflows/`) still runs the fuzzer and every
# check, and changing which checks run or are required stays owned.
